# Provenance — charts/README.md § `SOURCE` is required, including when nothing was forked.
#
# ADR-010: "Their charts are a starting point, not a dependency. Where a chart is close, fork it into
# charts/ with the upstream commit recorded in a SOURCE file." Nothing was forked here — Kube-OVN is
# platform infrastructure installed once per cluster (ADR-019) and what this chart renders is a
# tenant's row in that installation — and `vendored: none` is an answer to "where did this come from"
# where a missing file is not.

vendored: none
upstream: https://github.com/kubeovn/kube-ovn

# The upstream API this chart's template targets. ⚠ An API group/version, not a commit: nothing is
# vendored, so there is no upstream tree to pin.
upstream-api: kubeovn.io/v1

engine: https://github.com/ovn-org/ovn

See ../kube-ovn-vpc/SOURCE for the family's shared review of the Kube-OVN CRD set and
../kube-ovn-eip/SOURCE for the address this rule names. What was read at tag v1.16.2 for THIS chart,
and the facts that bear hardest on it:

  * pkg/apis/kubeovn/v1/ovn-snat-rule.go —
    `+kubebuilder:resource:scope="Cluster",shortName="osnat",path="ovn-snat-rules",singular="ovn-snat-rule"`.
    CLUSTER-SCOPED and the plural is HYPHENATED: the third such plural in this family, and the same
    trap — ClusterConformanceHarness derives its CRD stub's path from GroupVersionKind.Plural.
  * OvnSnatRuleSpec is six OPTIONAL strings — ovnEip, vpcSubnet, ipName, vpc, v4IpCidr, v6IpCidr.
    Checked in the CRD the chart installs: `spec` declares NO `required:` list and NO `default:`.
    This chart sends ovnEip, vpcSubnet and vpc and nothing else. ipName translates ONE pod's `IP`
    object and v4IpCidr/v6IpCidr a bare range; each would let a body reach a range this resource's
    address does not vouch for, so neither is expressible.
  * pkg/apis/kubeovn/v1/vpc-nat-gateway.go, which is the object docs/plan/14 names and this chart
    deliberately is not: "VpcNatGateway represents a NAT gateway for a VPC, implemented as a
    StatefulSet Pod", and its rules are IptablesSnatRules whose `spec.eip` names an IptablesEIP — a
    second public-address kind, allocated by that pod, which ../kube-ovn-eip does not render.
  * pkg/controller/ovn_snat.go `handleAddOvnSnatRule`: reads the OvnEip named by spec.ovnEip
    ("failed to get eip" and a retry when it does not exist), refuses an EIP of type `lsp`, resolves
    spec.vpcSubnet through the subnet lister to the subnet's `spec.vpc` and `spec.cidrBlock`
    ("failed to get vpc subnet" and a retry when it does not exist), then issues ONE
    `AddNat(vpc, SNAT, eip, cidr)` per family on the VPC's logical router. THE CONTROLLER NEVER
    WRITES BACK TO `.spec` ON THIS KIND — the first time that is true in this family. It patches a
    finalizer, the `ovn.kubernetes.io/eip_v4_ip`/`eip_v6_ip` labels, the `ovn.kubernetes.io/vpc_eip`
    annotation and the status subresource (ready, vpc, v4Eip, v6Eip, v4IpCidr, v6IpCidr).
  * It ALSO patches the ADDRESS: `natLabelAndAnnoOvnEip` writes the rule's name into the OvnEip's
    `ovn.kubernetes.io/vpc_nat` annotation and `patchOvnEipStatus` refreshes its status, which is what
    `POST …/publicIpAddresses/{name}/showAllocation` reports as `attachedTo`. So an address with a
    NAT gateway on it is the first address on this platform whose `attachedTo` is not empty.
  * `handleUpdateOvnSnatRule`: returns at "already ok" until status.ready; afterwards recomputes the
    VPC, both addresses and both CIDRs from the NEW spec and refuses every difference by name —
    "vpc changed", "v4 eip changed", "v6 eip changed", "v4 ip cidr changed", "v6 ip cidr changed".
    AN OvnSnatRule IS WHOLLY IMMUTABLE ONCE READY. conformance.yaml § owed,
    `a-nat-rule-cannot-be-changed`.
  * `enqueueUpdateOvnSnatRule` re-queues the OLD address for a reset when spec.ovnEip changes, so a
    changed address name is refused AND clears the old address's `status.nat` — the one visible effect
    an update has on a real fabric.
  * pkg/controller/ovn_eip.go `getOvnEipNat` lists dnat, fip and snat rules by the
    `ovn.kubernetes.io/eip_v4_ip` label and `handleUpdateOvnEip` refuses to release an address while
    any exist — "is still being used by NAT rules … waiting for them to be deleted". So SEVERAL
    OvnSnatRules may name one OvnEip, which is why several NAT gateways may share one address, and a
    publicIpAddresses delete waits for its NAT gateways.
  * pkg/daemon/gateway.go `isSubnetNeedNat` requires `subnet.Spec.Vpc == c.config.ClusterRouter`:
    the node-side masquerade behind Subnet.spec.natOutgoing serves the DEFAULT VPC only. Every
    subnet ../kube-ovn-subnet renders binds to a tenant Vpc, so `natOutgoing` there is a control the
    substrate ignores and THIS chart is a tenant subnet's only egress.
    ../kube-ovn-subnet/conformance.yaml § owed, `nat-outgoing-is-ignored-in-a-tenant-vpc`.
  * pkg/controller/vpc.go: the NAT row is programmed on the router regardless of
    Vpc.spec.enableExternal; packets leave through the external logical router port that flag
    attaches, and without it they are translated and dropped. Nothing here can read the network's
    flag. conformance.yaml § owed, `egress-needs-the-networks-external-attachment`.
